				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chaosera" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2227264); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2227264&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607374176" alt="ChaoSera" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2227264)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chaosera" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2227264); return false;" >ChaoSera</a></span> <span class="odate time_1482418662 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Dec 2016 14:57</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2713597">
									

<p>I really like this idea and was going to upvote. However, I'm not sure how I feel about the Foundation forcing people who are already covered under the insurance to end their policies. I'm assuming they did not know what they were getting into when they signed up and essentially sentencing a lot of them to a particular gruesome death does not sit right with me and seems unnecessarily cruel of the Foundation.</p>
<p>I get that the goal is to prevent the negative side effects of the insurance from affecting other people but you specify that regular interaction with the -B instance is necessary for these to occur. Two possible, more humane solutions come to mind for me. Either stick them in complete Isolation and have their needs like food etc. be met automatically. Of course there is an argument to be made for unending isolation being a fate that's possibly even worse than death. A different way to go would be to have a changing roster of D-Class provide their needs and some social interaction. Switch the D-Class out often enough so they don't suffer the side effects.</p>
<p>A third interesting option would be to see what happens when, instead of forcing them to cancel their policies, you make them move into an isolated &quot;colony&quot; of -B instances. Their respective policies should prevent the side effects from affecting the other inhabitants and they could possibly live an almost normal life.</p>
<p>For now, I'm sticking with a novote.</p>

